@page "/docs/concepts/scopes-claims"
@layout DocsLayout
@attribute [AllowAnonymous]

<PageTitle>Scopes &amp; claims — Vaultex</PageTitle>

<div class="docs-eyebrow">Concepts</div>
<h1>Scopes &amp; claims</h1>
<p class="docs-lead">
    Scopes are what your client <em>asks for</em>. Claims are what shows up <em>in the token</em>.
    This page maps each scope to the claims it includes and explains how to design custom ones.
</p>

<h2 id="standard-scopes">Standard scopes</h2>
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Scope</th><th>Adds these claims</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>openid</code></td><td><code>sub</code>. Required for any OIDC sign-in.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>profile</code></td><td><code>name</code>, <code>preferred_username</code>, <code>given_name</code>, <code>family_name</code>, <code>locale</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>email</code></td><td><code>email</code>, <code>email_verified</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>roles</code></td><td><code>role</code> (multi-valued, tenant-scoped)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>offline_access</code></td><td>(no claims) Issues a refresh token alongside the access token.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="claim-shapes">Claim shapes</h2>
<p>Vaultex follows the OIDC standard claim names. Here's a typical decoded ID token:</p>

<div class="docs-codeblock">
    <div class="docs-codeblock-header">
        <span class="docs-codeblock-lang">JSON</span>
        <button class="docs-codeblock-copy" onclick="vxCopy(this)">Copy</button>
    </div>
<pre><code>{
  "iss":            "https://auth.vaultex.io",
  "aud":            "your-client-id",
  "sub":            "f3a7d2c9-1b4e-4f0a-9c2e-1234567890ab",
  "name":           "Sarah Chen",
  "preferred_username": "sarah",
  "email":          "sarah@example.com",
  "email_verified": true,
  "tid":            "acme",
  "role":           ["Admin", "Viewer"],
  "iat":            1715900400,
  "exp":            1715904000
}</code></pre>
</div>

<h2 id="tenant-claim">The tenant claim</h2>
<p>
    Vaultex adds a <code>tid</code> claim to every token. It tells your app which tenant the user
    belongs to. Use it to scope database queries and feature flags:
</p>

<div class="docs-codeblock">
    <div class="docs-codeblock-header">
        <span class="docs-codeblock-lang">C#</span>
        <button class="docs-codeblock-copy" onclick="vxCopy(this)">Copy</button>
    </div>
<pre><code>var tenantId = User.FindFirst("tid")?.Value;
var orders = await _db.Orders
    .Where(o =&gt; o.TenantId == tenantId)
    .ToListAsync();</code></pre>
</div>

<h2 id="custom">Custom scopes</h2>
<p>
    Need to expose a custom permission like <code>billing:write</code>? Define it in the admin
    portal under <strong>Scopes</strong>, attach it to the relevant client, and your app can request
    it like any other:
</p>

<div class="docs-codeblock">
    <div class="docs-codeblock-header">
        <span class="docs-codeblock-lang">C#</span>
        <button class="docs-codeblock-copy" onclick="vxCopy(this)">Copy</button>
    </div>
<pre><code>options.Scope.Add("billing:write");</code></pre>
</div>

